On masters in business, State Street Investment Chief Lori Heinel recalls trading fixed income during the 1987 Black Monday crash.

58
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:41.560
It's a ground level look at market stress and how early career constraints can put someone inside a historic event.

59
00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:42.120
Sure.

60
00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.560
And I just found that fascinating.

61
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:51.560
I thought it was really um amazing to uh connect both you know what's going on in the world with you know how finance supports that.

62
00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:57.480
Um and so I did that for a couple of years, and then at the end of the two-year program, you're typically expected to go back to business school.

63
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:13.560
Well, I still needed to make money because I had student loans to pay off, so I decided I wanted to stay, and that let me led me to an opportunity on the trading desk at uh First Boston, which uh really was an incredible opportunity because that was my first real introduction to markets.

64
00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.320
So, what did working on the trading floor teach you about markets?

65
00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:19.080
So many things.

66
00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:28.200
Um I think the first and most important thing is I was there during the 87 uh Black Monday crash, and I happened to be working in fixed income.

67
00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:35.160
So it was a really interesting day because of course um at that time the first Boston trading floor was on two different levels.

68
00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.800
So you had all the fixed income was on one level, all the equities was on a different level.

69
00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:43.480
And we went dead silent in the first part of the day.

70
00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:49.160
And suddenly people were starting to realize what was happening, the market crashing, you know, 20 plus percent.

71
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:55.320
22 percent over the course of you know a day, which of course today we've got calibrators that don't let that happen anymore.

72
00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:16.640
But then all of a sudden, towards the end of the day, things on the fixed income market started going crazy because now you had the Fed coming out, Alan Greenspan saying, you know, we're gonna go ahead and provide liquidity, we're gonna make sure that there's, you know, active uh engagement to forestall any further, you know, recessions or other things that might be uh caused by this kind of major crash.

73
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:23.600
So I guess the first lesson I learned was that there are winners and there are losers in every market event.

74
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.520
And it's better to be on the winning side.

75
00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:29.600
So I happen to be at that time on the bond side, which was the big winner that day.

76
00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:40.960
Um but then I think the other thing that I learned was that you have to be really careful about things like moral hazard, because we became accustomed in that moment to this idea of the Fed put.

77
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:50.400
And I think many years later, uh, we are still wondering about you know what that really does mean in terms of the reaction function.

